@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.44}Exercise 4.44:} @ref{Exercise 2.42} described the
``eight-queens puzzle'' of placing queens on a chessboard so that no two attack
each other.  Write a nondeterministic program to solve this puzzle.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Parsing natural language

Programs designed to accept natural language as input usually start by
attempting to @newterm{parse} the input, that is, to match the input against
some grammatical structure.  For example, we might try to recognize simple
sentences consisting of an article followed by a noun followed by a verb, such
as ``The cat eats.''  To accomplish such an analysis, we must be able to
identify the parts of speech of individual words.  We could start with some
lists that classify various words:@footnote{Here we use the convention that the
first element of each list designates the part of speech for the rest of the
words in the list.}

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define nouns '(noun student professor cat class))",
    "(define verbs '(verb studies lectures eats sleeps))",
    "(define articles '(article the a))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
We also need a @newterm{grammar}, that is, a set of rules describing how
grammatical elements are composed from simpler elements.  A very simple grammar
might stipulate that a sentence always consists of two pieces---a noun phrase
followed by a verb---and that a noun phrase consists of an article followed by
a noun.  With this grammar, the sentence ``The cat eats'' is parsed as follows:

@example
(sentence
 (noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat))
 (verb eats))
@end example

@noindent
We can generate such a parse with a simple program that has separate procedures
for each of the grammatical rules.  To parse a sentence, we identify its two
constituent pieces and return a list of these two elements, tagged with the
symbol @code{sentence}:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-sentence) \
     (list 'sentence \
     (parse-noun-phrase) \
     (parse-word verbs)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
A noun phrase, similarly, is parsed by finding an article followed by a
noun:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-noun-phrase) \
     (list 'noun-phrase \
     (parse-word articles) \
     (parse-word nouns)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
At the lowest level, parsing boils down to repeatedly checking that the next
unparsed word is a member of the list of words for the required part of speech.
To implement this, we maintain a global variable @code{*unparsed*}, which is
the input that has not yet been parsed.  Each time we check a word, we require
that @code{*unparsed*} must be non-empty and that it should begin with a word
from the designated list.  If so, we remove that word from @code{*unparsed*}
and return the word together with its part of speech (which is found at the
head of the list):@footnote{Notice that @code{parse-word} uses @code{set!} to
modify the unparsed input list.  For this to work, our @code{amb} evaluator
must undo the effects of @code{set!} operations when it backtracks.}

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-word word-list) \
     (require (not (null? *unparsed*))) \
     (require (memq (car *unparsed*) (cdr word-list))) \
     (let ((found-word (car *unparsed*))) \
     (set! *unparsed* (cdr *unparsed*)) \
     (list (car word-list) found-word)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
To start the parsing, all we need to do is set @code{*unparsed*} to be the
entire input, try to parse a sentence, and check that nothing is left over:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define *unparsed* '())",
    "(define (parse input) \
     (set! *unparsed* input) \
     (let ((sent (parse-sentence))) \
     (require (null? *unparsed*)) \
     sent))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
We can now try the parser and verify that it works for our simple test
sentence:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(parse '(the cat eats))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Starting a new problem
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: (sentence
// =>  (noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat))
// =>  (verb eats))
@end example

@noindent
The @code{amb} evaluator is useful here because it is convenient to express the
parsing constraints with the aid of @code{require}.  Automatic search and
backtracking really pay off, however, when we consider more complex grammars
where there are choices for how the units can be decomposed.

Let's add to our grammar a list of prepositions:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define prepositions '(prep for to in by with))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
and define a prepositional phrase (e.g., ``for the cat'') to be a preposition
followed by a noun phrase:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-prepositional-phrase) \
     (list 'prep-phrase \
     (parse-word prepositions) \
     (parse-noun-phrase)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
Now we can define a sentence to be a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase,
where a verb phrase can be either a verb or a verb phrase extended by a
prepositional phrase:@footnote{Observe that this definition is recursive---a
verb may be followed by any number of prepositional phrases.}

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-sentence) \
     (list 'sentence \
     (parse-noun-phrase) \
     (parse-verb-phrase)))",
    "(define (parse-verb-phrase) \
     (define (maybe-extend verb-phrase) \
     (amb verb-phrase \
     (maybe-extend \
     (list 'verb-phrase \
     verb-phrase \
     (parse-prepositional-phrase))))) \
     (maybe-extend (parse-word verbs)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
While we're at it, we can also elaborate the definition of noun phrases to
permit such things as ``a cat in the class.''  What we used to call a noun
phrase, we'll now call a simple noun phrase, and a noun phrase will now be
either a simple noun phrase or a noun phrase extended by a prepositional
phrase:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(define (parse-simple-noun-phrase) \
     (list 'simple-noun-phrase \
     (parse-word articles) \
     (parse-word nouns)))",
    "(define (parse-noun-phrase) \
     (define (maybe-extend noun-phrase) \
     (amb noun-phrase \
     (maybe-extend \
     (list 'noun-phrase \
     noun-phrase \
     (parse-prepositional-phrase))))) \
     (maybe-extend (parse-simple-noun-phrase)))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: ok
@end example

@noindent
Our new grammar lets us parse more complex sentences.  For example

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(parse '(the student with the cat \
     sleeps in the class))",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Starting a new problem
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: (sentence
// =>  (noun-phrase
// =>   (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun student))
// =>   (prep-phrase (prep with)
// =>                (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                 (article the)
// =>                 (noun cat))))
// =>  (verb-phrase
// =>   (verb sleeps)
// =>   (prep-phrase (prep in)
// =>                (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                 (article the)
// =>                 (noun class)))))
@end example

@noindent
Observe that a given input may have more than one legal parse.  In the sentence
``The professor lectures to the student with the cat,'' it may be that the
professor is lecturing with the cat, or that the student has the cat.  Our
nondeterministic program finds both possibilities:

@example rust
let session = amb_session(&[
    "(parse '(the professor lectures to \
     the student with the cat))",
    "try-again",
]);
println!("@{session@}");
// => ;;; Starting a new problem
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: (sentence
// =>  (simple-noun-phrase (article the)
// =>                      (noun professor))
// =>  (verb-phrase
// =>   (verb-phrase
// =>    (verb lectures)
// =>    (prep-phrase (prep to)
// =>                 (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                  (article the)
// =>                  (noun student))))
// =>   (prep-phrase (prep with)
// =>                (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                 (article the)
// =>                 (noun cat)))))
// => ;;; Amb-Eval value: (sentence
// =>  (simple-noun-phrase (article the)
// =>                      (noun professor))
// =>  (verb-phrase (verb lectures)
// =>               (prep-phrase
// =>                (prep to)
// =>                (noun-phrase
// =>                 (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                  (article the)
// =>                  (noun student))
// =>                 (prep-phrase
// =>                  (prep with)
// =>                  (simple-noun-phrase
// =>                   (article the)
// =>                   (noun cat)))))))
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.45}Exercise 4.45:} With the grammar given above, the
following sentence can be parsed in five different ways: ``The professor
lectures to the student in the class with the cat.''  Give the five parses and
explain the differences in shades of meaning among them.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.46}Exercise 4.46:} The evaluators in
@ref{4.1} and @ref{4.2} do not determine what order operands are evaluated in.
We will see that the @code{amb} evaluator evaluates them from left to right.
Explain why our parsing program wouldn't work if the operands were evaluated in
some other order.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.47}Exercise 4.47:} Louis Reasoner suggests that,
since a verb phrase is either a verb or a verb phrase followed by a
prepositional phrase, it would be much more straightforward to define the
procedure @code{parse-verb-phrase} as follows (and similarly for noun phrases):

@example
(define (parse-verb-phrase)
  (amb (parse-word verbs)
       (list 
        'verb-phrase
        (parse-verb-phrase)
        (parse-prepositional-phrase))))
@end example

Does this work?  Does the program's behavior change if we interchange the order
of expressions in the @code{amb}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.48}Exercise 4.48:} Extend the grammar given above to
handle more complex sentences.  For example, you could extend noun phrases and
verb phrases to include adjectives and adverbs, or you could handle compound
sentences.@footnote{This kind of grammar can become arbitrarily complex, but it
is only a toy as far as real language understanding is concerned.  Real
natural-language understanding by computer requires an elaborate mixture of
syntactic analysis and interpretation of meaning.  On the other hand, even toy
parsers can be useful in supporting flexible command languages for programs
such as information-retrieval systems.  @ref{Winston 1992} discusses computational
approaches to real language understanding and also the applications of simple
grammars to command languages.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.49}Exercise 4.49:} Alyssa P. Hacker is more
interested in generating interesting sentences than in parsing them.  She
reasons that by simply changing the procedure @code{parse-word} so that it
ignores the ``input sentence'' and instead always succeeds and generates an
appropriate word, we can use the programs we had built for parsing to do
generation instead.  Implement Alyssa's idea, and show the first half-dozen or
so sentences generated.@footnote{Although Alyssa's idea works just fine (and is
surprisingly simple), the sentences that it generates are a bit boring---they
don't sample the possible sentences of this language in a very interesting way.
In fact, the grammar is highly recursive in many places, and Alyssa's technique
``falls into'' one of these recursions and gets stuck.  See @ref{Exercise 4.50}
for a way to deal with this.}
@end quotation

@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the @code{Amb} Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary Scheme expression may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal an error.  In nondeterministic Scheme the evaluation
of an expression may in addition result in the discovery of a dead end, in
which case evaluation must backtrack to a previous choice point.  The
interpretation of nondeterministic Scheme is complicated by this extra case.

We will construct the @code{amb} evaluator for nondeterministic Scheme by
building an explicit backtracking engine on the evaluator substrate of
@ref{4.1}.@footnote{We chose to
implement the lazy evaluator in @ref{4.2} as a modification of the
ordinary metacircular evaluator of @ref{4.1.1}.  In this edition the
@code{amb} evaluator is likewise plain code rather than continuation
passing: a self-referential continuation closure is painful to build in
Rust, while a typed failure signal unwinding to a stored choice frame is
plain, testable, and semantically equivalent -- the book's fail
continuation is the engine's saved frame.}  As in the analyzing
evaluator, evaluation of an expression is accomplished by handing each
subexpression's value to a continuation that runs the rest of the
computation.  The difference between the
interpretation of ordinary Scheme and the interpretation of nondeterministic
Scheme is that the rest of the computation is stored data: a choice frame
keeps its pending alternatives together with the continuation that consumes
each chosen value, so a failure can resume the frame and a returned answer
can leave the frame resumable.

@subsubheading Continuations and failure

The book's @code{amb} evaluator threads two @newterm{continuation
procedures} through every execution procedure: a @newterm{success
continuation}, called with each value the evaluation produces, and a
@newterm{failure continuation}, called when the evaluation discovers a dead
end.  The edition keeps the success continuation and stores the failure
machinery in the engine: the failure path is a typed signal plus the saved
choice frames, so no second closure kind is threaded through the program.
The success continuation is one type:

@example rust
/// The rest of one expression's evaluation: the book's success
/// continuation, a closure from the value just obtained to everything
/// that follows it.
pub type Cont = Rc<dyn Fn(&Amb, Value) -> EvalResult>;
@end example

@noindent
A failure needs no closure: it is the typed signal

@example rust
/// The engine's failure signal: the book's @code{(amb)} and a
/// @code{require} that does not hold both raise it, and the nearest
/// choice frame catches it.
pub fn fail(&self) -> EvalResult {
    Err(SchemeError::Backtrack)
}
@end example

@noindent
which unwinds the host stack to the nearest choice frame.  The frames live
in the evaluator:

@example rust
/// One resumable choice frame: the book's @code{try-next} loop stored
/// as data. @code{alts} holds the alternatives not yet tried, each with
/// the environment to evaluate it in; @code{k} is the continuation that
/// consumes each chosen value; @code{trail_len} is the trail length at
/// the push, the line backtracking rolls the trail back to.
struct AmbFrame {
    alts: VecDeque<(Value, Rc<Env>)>,
    k: Cont,
    trail_len: usize,
}
@end example

@noindent
Because a solution that has returned to the driver no longer has a host
stack under it, the frame keeps the continuation: @code{try_again} can
re-enter the rest of the computation from the frame alone, which is what
makes the driver's second answer resume instead of replay.

Failures are initiated only when a dead end is encountered.  This occurs

@itemize @bullet

@item
if the user program executes @code{(amb)} -- an expression that always fails,
the device @code{require} is built from (see @ref{4.3.1});

@item
if the user types @code{try-again} at the top-level driver.

@end itemize

@noindent
In addition, if a side-effect operation (such as assignment to a variable)
occurs on a branch of the process resulting from a choice, it may be necessary,
when the process finds a dead end, to undo the side effect before making a new
choice.  This is accomplished by an undo trail: every assignment records the
frame, the name, and the old value, and backtracking rolls the trail back to
the line it was at when the owning frame was pushed.

@subsubheading Structure of the evaluator

The syntax- and data-representation procedures for the @code{amb} evaluator
are identical to those in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, and the dispatch
below reads like that evaluator's clause chain, except for the fact
that we need additional syntax procedures to recognize the new special
forms:@footnote{We assume
that the evaluator supports @code{let} (see @ref{Exercise 4.22}), which we have
used in our nondeterministic programs.  The base language installs
@code{require} as an ordinary procedure, so the dispatch below has no
@code{require} clause; exercise @ref{Exercise 4.54} installs one.}

@example rust
if is_tagged_list(exp, "amb") @{
    return self.search(exp, env, k, Search::InOrder);
@}
if is_tagged_list(exp, "ramb") @{
    return self.search(exp, env, k, Search::Shuffled);
@}
if is_assignment(exp) @{
    return self.eval_assignment(exp, env, &k, Assignment::Undoable);
@}
if is_tagged_list(exp, "permanent-set!") @{
    return self.eval_assignment(exp, env, &k, Assignment::Permanent);
@}
if is_tagged_list(exp, "if-fail") @{
    return self.eval_if_fail(exp, env, &k);
@}
@end example

@noindent
The top-level entry @code{eval_with} (similar to the version of @code{eval}
given in @ref{4.1.7}) analyzes nothing at driver time -- the dispatch runs
directly over the syntax -- but like the analyzed evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}
it answers through an execution procedure: here, the continuation that
receives the value and runs the rest of the computation.  In going through
the clauses, compare each with the corresponding procedure for the ordinary
evaluator given in @ref{4.1.7}:

@example rust
pub fn eval_with(&self, exp: &Value, env: &Rc<Env>, k: Cont) -> EvalResult
@end example

@noindent
A success continuation is a closure of one value argument: the value just
obtained.  So the general form of the engine's clause shape is

@example rust
// @code{k} is @code{Rc<dyn Fn(&Amb, Value) -> EvalResult>}
self.eval_with(&value_expression, env, cont(move |amb, value| @{
    // @code{value} is this form's answer; @code{k} runs the rest
    k(amb, @r{…})
@}))
@end example

@noindent
For example, executing

@example rust
let answer: Cont = cont(|_, value| Ok(value));
amb.eval_with(&form, &env, answer)
@end example

@noindent
will attempt to evaluate the given form and will return either the form's
value (if the evaluation succeeds) or the @code{Backtrack} signal (if the
evaluation fails).  The driver loop shown below uses this entry with a
frame stack it keeps between inputs, which is what supports the
@code{try-again} request.

@subsubheading Simple expressions

The clauses for the simplest kinds of expressions are essentially the same
as those for the ordinary evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, except for handing the
value on through the continuation instead of returning it:

@example rust
if is_self_evaluating(exp) @{
    return k(self, exp.clone());
@}
if is_quoted(exp) @{
    return k(self, text_of_quotation(exp)?);
@}
if is_variable(exp) @{
    return k(self, lookup_variable(exp, env)?);
@}
if is_lambda(exp) @{
    return k(self, Value::Closure(lambda_closure(exp, env)?));
@}
@end example

@noindent
Notice that looking up a variable always `succeeds.'  If the lookup fails
to find the variable, it signals an error, as usual.  Such a ``failure''
indicates a program bug---a reference to an unbound variable; it is not an
indication that we should try another nondeterministic choice instead of
the one that is currently being tried.

@subsubheading Conditionals and sequences

Conditionals evaluate the predicate against a continuation that picks the
branch, so a choice inside the predicate resumes into the branch it
chose:

@example rust
fn eval_if(&self, exp: &Value, env: &Rc<Env>, k: &Cont) -> EvalResult @{
    let (predicate, consequent, alternative) = if_parts(exp)?;
    let branch: Cont = @{
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, tested| @{
            let chosen = if is_true(&tested) @{
                consequent.clone()
            @} else @{
                alternative.clone()
            @};
            amb.eval_with(&chosen, &env, Rc::clone(&k))
        @})
    @};
    self.eval_with(&predicate, env, branch)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Sequences run each form but the last against a continuation that runs the
rest, so a choice inside an early form resumes into the later forms:

@example rust
pub fn eval_sequence(&self, forms: &[Value], env: &Rc<Env>, k: &Cont) -> EvalResult @{
    let Some((head, tail)) = forms.split_first() else @{
        return Err(SchemeError::TypeMismatch(
            "Empty sequence: ANALYZE".to_owned(),
        ));
    @};
    if tail.is_empty() @{
        return self.eval_with(head, env, Rc::clone(k));
    @}
    let rest: Cont = @{
        let tail = tail.to_vec();
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, _| amb.eval_sequence(&tail, &env, Rc::clone(&k)))
    @};
    self.eval_with(head, env, rest)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Definitions and assignments

Definitions evaluate the definition-value expression first; the
continuation binds the name and answers @code{ok}:

@example rust
fn eval_definition(&self, exp: &Value, env: &Rc<Env>, k: &Cont) -> EvalResult @{
    let name = variable_name(&definition_variable(exp)?)?;
    let value_exp = definition_value(exp)?;
    let bind: Cont = @{
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, value| @{
            env.define(Rc::clone(&name), value);
            k(amb, Value::sym("ok"))
        @})
    @};
    self.eval_with(&value_exp, env, bind)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Assignments are more interesting.  This is the first place where the undo
trail earns its keep.  The assignment clause's continuation saves the old
value of the variable, assigns, and -- for the undoable kind -- records the
entry that backtracking restores.  A successful assignment therefore
provides, as data, everything a subsequent failure needs to undo it before
the search proceeds.@footnote{We didn't worry about undoing
definitions, since we can assume that internal definitions are scanned out
(@ref{4.1.6}).}

@example rust
fn eval_assignment(
    &self,
    exp: &Value,
    env: &Rc<Env>,
    k: &Cont,
    kind: Assignment,
) -> EvalResult @{
    let name = variable_name(&assignment_variable(exp)?)?;
    let value_exp = assignment_value(exp)?;
    let assign: Cont = @{
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, value| @{
            let old = env.lookup(&name)?;
            env.set(&name, value)?;
            if kind == Assignment::Undoable @{
                amb.record_undo(&env, &name, old);
            @}
            k(amb, Value::sym("ok"))
        @})
    @};
    self.eval_with(&value_exp, env, assign)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The trail rolls back in one place.  When a search unwinds to a choice
frame -- a failed alternative replay, or the driver's @code{try_again} --
the engine restores every entry above the frame's push line:

@example rust
fn undo_to(&self, limit: usize) @{
    let mut trail = self.trail.borrow_mut();
    while trail.len() > limit @{
        let Some(undo) = trail.pop() else @{
            break;
        @};
        undo.env
            .set(&undo.name, undo.old)
            .expect("trail entry restores its own binding");
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Procedure applications

The application clause contains no new ideas except for the bookkeeping
that makes the operand walk resumable.  The operator evaluates first, then
the operands walk left to right, each against a continuation that carries
the operator, the arguments already obtained, and the operand expressions
still to evaluate:

@example rust
fn eval_application(&self, exp: &Value, env: &Rc<Env>, k: &Cont) -> EvalResult @{
    let operator = first_of(exp)?;
    let operands = operand_items(exp)?;
    let walk: Cont = @{
        let operands = operands.clone();
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, proc| @{
            continue_args(amb, proc, &[], &operands, &env, &k)
        @})
    @};
    self.eval_with(&operator, env, walk)
@}

fn continue_args(
    amb: &Amb,
    proc: Value,
    done: &[Value],
    rest: &[Value],
    env: &Rc<Env>,
    k: &Cont,
) -> EvalResult @{
    let Some((head, tail)) = rest.split_first() else @{
        return amb.apply_procedure(&proc, done, k);
    @};
    let step: Cont = @{
        let done = done.to_vec();
        let tail = tail.to_vec();
        let env = Rc::clone(env);
        let k = Rc::clone(k);
        cont(move |amb, value| @{
            let mut args = done.clone();
            args.push(value);
            continue_args(amb, proc.clone(), &args, &tail, &env, &k)
        @})
    @};
    amb.eval_with(head, env, step)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The actual procedure application is performed in the same way as for the
ordinary evaluator, except that a compound procedure's body runs against
the incoming continuation instead of returning to a caller's stack frame:

@example rust
pub fn apply_procedure(&self, proc: &Value, args: &[Value], k: &Cont) -> EvalResult @{
    match proc @{
        Value::Primitive @{ f, .. @} => k(self, f(args)?),
        Value::Closure(closure) => @{
            let name = closure.name.as_deref().unwrap_or("#[compound-procedure]");
            let frame = extend_environment(
                name,
                &closure.params,
                closure.rest.as_ref(),
                args,
                &closure.env,
            )?;
            self.eval_sequence(&closure.body, &frame, k)
        @}
        other => Err(SchemeError::NotProcedure(other.clone())),
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating @code{amb} expressions

The @code{amb} special form is the key element in the nondeterministic
language.  Its clause pushes one frame of pending alternatives and drives
it: the drive loop evaluates each front alternative against the frame's
continuation, retries through the @code{Backtrack} signal, and unwinds --
popping its own frame and rolling back its trail segment -- when the
alternatives run dry.  The unwind is what makes the @code{amb} site that
pushed this frame try its next alternative.

@example rust
fn search(&self, exp: &Value, env: &Rc<Env>, k: Cont, order: Search) -> EvalResult @{
    let choices = operand_items(exp)?;
    let mut alts: Vec<_> = choices
        .iter()
        .map(|choice| (choice.clone(), Rc::clone(env)))
        .collect();
    if order == Search::Shuffled @{
        let mut rng = self.rng.borrow_mut();
        for i in (1..alts.len()).rev() @{
            let bound = u64::try_from(i + 1).expect("an alternative count fits u64");
            let draw = rng.random(bound);
            let j = usize::try_from(draw).expect("the draw lands below the count");
            alts.swap(i, j);
        @}
    @}
    self.push_frame(alts, k);
    self.drive()
@}

pub fn drive(&self) -> EvalResult @{
    while let Some(job) = self.next_job() @{
        match job @{
            Job::Alternative @{ expr, env, k, trail_len @} => @{
                // A no-op for the first alternative (the trail ends at
                // the push line) and the rollback of the failed
                // alternative's mutations for every later one.
                self.undo_to(trail_len);
                let result = self.eval_with(&expr, &env, k);
                if let Err(SchemeError::Backtrack) = result @{
                    self.count_failure();
                    self.undo_to(trail_len);
                @} else @{
                    return result;
                @}
            @}
            Job::Exhausted(frame) => @{
                self.count_failure();
                self.undo_to(frame.trail_len);
                return Err(SchemeError::Backtrack);
            @}
        @}
    @}
    Err(SchemeError::Backtrack)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Driver loop

The driver loop for the @code{amb} evaluator is complex, due to the mechanism
that permits the user to try again in evaluating an expression.  In the
edition the driver is the evaluator itself: @code{run} starts a new problem,
discarding the unexplored alternatives of any earlier one, and
@code{try_again} yields the next answer of the problem in flight.  The
@code{try_again} loop is the book's @code{internal-loop}: it takes the
deepest pending frame's next alternative, evaluates it against the frame's
stored continuation -- the rest of the computation as data -- and so
resumes without replaying the work earlier answers already did.

@example rust
pub fn try_again(&self) -> EvalResult @{
    while let Some(job) = self.next_job() @{
        match job @{
            Job::Alternative @{ expr, env, k, trail_len @} => @{
                // Rolling the trail back to the frame's push line
                // discards the mutations of the path this resume
                // leaves behind -- the answered path, or the failed
                // alternative before it.
                self.undo_to(trail_len);
                let result = self.eval_with(&expr, &env, k);
                if let Err(SchemeError::Backtrack) = result @{
                    self.count_failure();
                    self.undo_to(trail_len);
                @} else @{
                    return result;
                @}
            @}
            Job::Exhausted(frame) => @{
                self.count_failure();
                self.undo_to(frame.trail_len);
            @}
        @}
    @}
    Err(SchemeError::Backtrack)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The initial call has no pending frame: a @code{try-again} with no problem
in flight reports exactly that, the book's ``There is no current
problem.''  When the deepest frame runs dry and every frame above it has
popped, @code{try_again} answers the same @code{Backtrack} signal the
driver prints as ``There are no more values of.''

